Read-only access to a TPC-H sales warehouse (customers, orders, line items, nations,
regions, suppliers, parts). Prefer the named catalog queries. If you do not already know
a query's parameters, call list_queries and describe_query first, then run_query.
Dates are ISO strings (YYYY-MM-DD); end_date is exclusive, so all of 1995 is
start_date 1995-01-01, end_date 1996-01-01. Results are capped by a row limit;
`truncated: true` means there may be more rows.

Catalog (catalog://queries):
{
  "queries": [
    {
      "name": "daily_order_volume",
      "description": "Daily order count and value, optionally restricted to one market segment.",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "start_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "end_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "segment",
          "type": "enum",
          "required": false,
          "default": "ALL",
          "values": [
            "ALL",
            "AUTOMOBILE",
            "BUILDING",
            "FURNITURE",
            "HOUSEHOLD",
            "MACHINERY"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "late_shipments",
      "description": "Share of line items received after their committed date, by order priority.",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "start_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "end_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "revenue_by_dimension",
      "description": "Net revenue and order count in a date window, grouped by one allowlisted dimension.",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "start_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true,
          "description": "Inclusive lower bound on o_orderdate"
        },
        {
          "name": "end_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true,
          "description": "Exclusive upper bound on o_orderdate"
        },
        {
          "name": "dimension",
          "type": "identifier",
          "required": false,
          "default": "n_name",
          "values": [
            "n_name",
            "r_name",
            "c_mktsegment",
            "o_orderpriority",
            "l_shipmode"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "top_customers",
      "description": "Highest-revenue customers in a region and date window. Row cap comes from the guard layer, not the SQL.",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "region",
          "type": "enum",
          "required": true,
          "values": [
            "AFRICA",
            "AMERICA",
            "ASIA",
            "EUROPE",
            "MIDDLE EAST"
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "start_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "end_date",
          "type": "date",
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "name": "min_orders",
          "type": "int",
          "required": false,
          "default": 1,
          "minimum": 1,
          "maximum": 1000
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Answer the user's question by calling run_query. You may call list_queries or describe_query first if you need a parameter.

